1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,962
Session six.

2
00:00:01,242 --> 00:00:02,562
Abusing CDNs.

3
00:00:02,842 --> 00:00:05,376
Someone else's computers, closer to your users,

4
00:00:05,376 --> 00:00:08,827
and everything they can do while the
traffic is passing through.

5
00:00:09,766 --> 00:00:11,417
First, why the edge exists.

6
00:00:11,697 --> 00:00:12,376
Physics.

7
00:00:12,656 --> 00:00:15,856
The speed of light is the one
number you cannot optimise.

8
00:00:16,199 --> 00:00:21,905
Light in a vacuum travels at two ninety-nine
thousand seven ninety-two kilometres a second.

9
00:00:22,185 --> 00:00:27,270
In fibre, about two thirds of that: two hundred
four thousand kilometres a second,

10
00:00:27,270 --> 00:00:30,185
a refractive index of one point four six eight.

11
00:00:30,465 --> 00:00:34,307
That is four point nine milliseconds per
thousand kilometres, one way.

12
00:00:35,333 --> 00:00:39,021
Mumbai to Chennai and back: about ten
milliseconds as a floor.

13
00:00:39,301 --> 00:00:40,947
A measured ping is twenty-three.

14
00:00:41,266 --> 00:00:46,478
Mumbai to Ashburn, Virginia, and back: one
hundred twenty-six milliseconds as a floor.

15
00:00:46,758 --> 00:00:48,283
You can never go below that.

16
00:00:48,633 --> 00:00:52,767
And TLS one point three needs three
round trips to the first byte.

17
00:00:53,047 --> 00:00:57,093
That is about three hundred eighty
milliseconds of nothing to Virginia.

18
00:00:57,373 --> 00:00:58,672
No CPU helps.

19
00:00:58,999 --> 00:01:02,066
Hollow core fibre would be forty-
seven percent faster.

20
00:01:02,346 --> 00:01:06,490
Microsoft Azure started deploying it in
September twenty twenty-five.

21
00:01:06,770 --> 00:01:08,266
It is not on your path today.

22
00:01:09,199 --> 00:01:12,624
Distance charges you twice: in
latency, and in loss.

23
00:01:12,904 --> 00:01:15,023
This is Akamai data from twenty ten.

24
00:01:16,066 --> 00:01:21,249
Local, under a hundred miles: one point six
milliseconds, point six percent loss,

25
00:01:21,249 --> 00:01:25,599
forty-four megabits, and a four gigabyte
download in twelve minutes.

26
00:01:25,933 --> 00:01:30,146
Regional: sixteen milliseconds, four
megabits, two point two hours.

27
00:01:30,466 --> 00:01:35,095
Cross-continent: forty-eight milliseconds,
one megabit, eight point two hours.

28
00:01:35,433 --> 00:01:41,432
Multi-continent: ninety-six milliseconds, one point
four percent loss, point four megabits,

29
00:01:41,432 --> 00:01:42,553
and twenty hours.

30
00:01:42,899 --> 00:01:45,863
The Mathis formula, from nineteen ninety-seven:

31
00:01:45,863 --> 00:01:48,827
throughput is roughly the maximum segment size,

32
00:01:48,827 --> 00:01:52,926
divided by the round trip time times
the square root of the loss.

33
00:01:53,206 --> 00:01:59,328
A single TCP flow's ceiling falls with both the
round trip time and the square root of loss.

34
00:01:59,608 --> 00:02:03,737
So the far edge is a hundred and ten
times slower, not sixty times.

35
00:02:04,017 --> 00:02:09,180
HTTP two multiplexing and BBR exist
to claw some of this back.

36
00:02:10,099 --> 00:02:11,886
What does an edge actually buy back?

37
00:02:12,166 --> 00:02:14,966
You are ten milliseconds from a Mumbai edge,

38
00:02:14,966 --> 00:02:19,486
which is one hundred ninety-four
milliseconds from a Washington origin,

39
00:02:19,486 --> 00:02:21,905
with TLS one point three on every hop.

40
00:02:22,233 --> 00:02:26,742
Direct to the origin on a new connection: six
hundred twenty-one milliseconds.

41
00:02:27,022 --> 00:02:28,065
Three round trips.

42
00:02:28,399 --> 00:02:30,698
Direct, kept alive: two hundred eight.

43
00:02:30,978 --> 00:02:31,970
One round trip.

44
00:02:32,299 --> 00:02:36,773
An edge with no origin pool, just a
longer proxy: six twenty-five.

45
00:02:37,053 --> 00:02:37,838
No better.

46
00:02:38,166 --> 00:02:41,223
An edge with a warm origin pool: two thirty-three.

47
00:02:41,566 --> 00:02:45,716
Warm pool and kept alive: two oh nine,
about the same as direct.

48
00:02:46,066 --> 00:02:48,571
An edge cache hit: thirty-four milliseconds.

49
00:02:48,899 --> 00:02:51,617
And a hit on a kept alive connection: eleven.

50
00:02:51,966 --> 00:02:56,506
A cache miss through the edge is no faster than
going direct, and sometimes slower.

51
00:02:56,786 --> 00:03:02,500
The edge earns its place in two ways: warm
connections to the origin, which save handshakes,

52
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:05,667
and cache hits, which delete
the origin round trip.

53
00:03:05,947 --> 00:03:08,766
One hundred ninety-four milliseconds become zero.

54
00:03:09,699 --> 00:03:11,051
So what is a CDN?

55
00:03:11,331 --> 00:03:15,264
Session four's reverse proxy, rented in
three hundred plus cities.

56
00:03:15,599 --> 00:03:16,093
You,

57
00:03:16,433 --> 00:03:21,244
ten milliseconds from an edge point of
presence, with cache, TLS and a WAF,

58
00:03:21,566 --> 00:03:23,681
which talks to a shield, or tiered cache,

59
00:03:23,999 --> 00:03:27,463
which talks to your origin, over
one long, controlled leg.

60
00:03:28,499 --> 00:03:29,682
Nothing here is new.

61
00:03:29,962 --> 00:03:33,023
The reverse proxy is nginx from session four.

62
00:03:33,366 --> 00:03:34,947
The caching is proxy_cache.

63
00:03:35,266 --> 00:03:38,067
The pool is upstream keepalive,
from session three.

64
00:03:38,399 --> 00:03:41,410
And anycast puts one IP address in every city.

65
00:03:41,690 --> 00:03:45,776
Squid, by the way, did reverse proxy
caching in nineteen ninety-six.

66
00:03:46,099 --> 00:03:49,088
What geography adds: cheap
handshakes near the user.

67
00:03:49,368 --> 00:03:54,240
The CDN owns both ends of the long leg, so
it controls transport and pooling.

68
00:03:54,520 --> 00:03:57,947
A shield layer means many edges
share one origin fetch.

69
00:03:58,227 --> 00:04:00,946
And one anycast address announced from every city.

70
00:04:01,866 --> 00:04:05,403
The founding algorithm of CDNs
is consistent hashing.

71
00:04:05,683 --> 00:04:08,650
Karger and colleagues, STOC nineteen ninety-seven.

72
00:04:08,930 --> 00:04:10,420
Akamai was founded on it.

73
00:04:10,766 --> 00:04:14,627
The problem: a hundred thousand URLs
spread over ten caches.

74
00:04:14,907 --> 00:04:16,118
Now add an eleventh.

75
00:04:17,166 --> 00:04:23,555
With hash of the URL modulo N, adding a server
moves ninety point nine percent of the URLs.

76
00:04:23,835 --> 00:04:27,303
That is a near-total cache flush,
and an origin stampede.

77
00:04:27,633 --> 00:04:32,896
With consistent hashing and one point per server,
only three point three percent move,

78
00:04:32,896 --> 00:04:37,548
but the load is uneven: the busiest server
gets nearly three times the mean.

79
00:04:37,866 --> 00:04:42,400
With a hundred sixty virtual points per
server, about one eleventh move,

80
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:46,053
exactly the new server's fair
share, and the load is even:

81
00:04:46,053 --> 00:04:48,194
one point one four times the mean.

82
00:04:49,133 --> 00:04:50,316
Let me show it on a ring.

83
00:04:51,666 --> 00:04:52,635
Imagine a ring.

84
00:04:52,915 --> 00:04:55,496
Four cache servers sit at hashed positions on it.

85
00:04:55,776 --> 00:04:58,797
Each URL is also hashed to a point on the ring,

86
00:04:58,797 --> 00:05:02,268
and belongs to the first server
it meets going around.

87
00:05:02,548 --> 00:05:04,102
The dots are colored by owner.

88
00:05:04,433 --> 00:05:06,020
Now add a fifth server, E.

89
00:05:06,300 --> 00:05:10,429
Only the keys between its predecessor and
its own position change owner.

90
00:05:10,709 --> 00:05:12,118
Everyone else stays put.

91
00:05:12,398 --> 00:05:13,592
That is the whole trick.

92
00:05:13,933 --> 00:05:15,188
Modulo is a trap.

93
00:05:15,468 --> 00:05:20,684
Consistent hashing moves only the new server's
fair share, about one over N plus one.

94
00:05:20,964 --> 00:05:23,865
Virtual points, many per
server, even out the load.

95
00:05:24,145 --> 00:05:30,726
nginx offers this as hash, request URI, consistent,
with a hundred sixty points per server,

96
00:05:30,726 --> 00:05:32,389
compatible with ketama.

97
00:05:32,669 --> 00:05:38,812
The same idea powers DHTs and sharded caches:
choosing an owner without a central registry.

98
00:05:39,733 --> 00:05:40,749
Who are the players?

99
00:05:41,766 --> 00:05:43,473
Akamai, nineteen ninety-eight.

100
00:05:43,753 --> 00:05:45,311
Its own ghost edge servers.

101
00:05:45,591 --> 00:05:50,355
It gave us ESI in two thousand one,
moved from FastTCP to BBR,

102
00:05:50,355 --> 00:05:53,275
and was founded on consistent hashing.

103
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:57,519
Amazon CloudFront, two thousand
eight, on the AWS edge.

104
00:05:57,799 --> 00:06:02,798
It has used BBR to viewers since twenty
nineteen, for up to twenty-two percent more.

105
00:06:03,133 --> 00:06:07,766
Fastly, two thousand eleven, a heavily
modified Varnish, with instant purge.

106
00:06:08,100 --> 00:06:12,521
Google's Media CDN, twenty twenty-two:
YouTube's edge, opened up.

107
00:06:12,866 --> 00:06:17,427
Broadpeak, born of video: an operator
or telco CDN for streaming.

108
00:06:17,707 --> 00:06:19,320
We will meet it in session eight.

109
00:06:19,666 --> 00:06:24,290
Cloudflare, two thousand ten, was
nginx plus LuaJIT, then Rust.

110
00:06:24,570 --> 00:06:28,970
Its second generation, FL two, shipped
in October twenty twenty-five,

111
00:06:28,970 --> 00:06:32,477
ten milliseconds faster and
twenty-five percent better.

112
00:06:32,757 --> 00:06:36,420
The origin facing proxy is Pingora,
from twenty twenty-two.

113
00:06:37,366 --> 00:06:39,440
Anycast, which you can see for yourself.

114
00:06:39,766 --> 00:06:43,268
Curl the trace endpoint, and it
prints colo equals BOM.

115
00:06:43,548 --> 00:06:51,094
Colo is an IATA airport code: BOM is Mumbai,
DEL is Delhi, MAA Chennai, SIN, LHR,

116
00:06:51,094 --> 00:06:52,584
IAD for Ashburn.

117
00:06:52,864 --> 00:06:59,796
It also shows TLS one point three, and the key
exchange X twenty-five five nineteen ML KEM,

118
00:06:59,796 --> 00:07:01,472
which is post-quantum.

119
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:06,393
A dig for CHAOS TXT id dot server
shows which resolver answered.

120
00:07:06,733 --> 00:07:11,061
And every response carries a cf-ray
header whose suffix is the colo.

121
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:15,027
The PoP is chosen by BGP anycast, not by you.

122
00:07:15,307 --> 00:07:19,831
Classroom Wi-Fi and mobile data give a
different colo for the same IP.

123
00:07:20,766 --> 00:07:21,634
Now caching.

124
00:07:21,914 --> 00:07:23,077
First, the defaults.

125
00:07:23,400 --> 00:07:31,970
Cloudflare caches by file extension: sixty-eight
static extensions, like css, js, png, jpg,

126
00:07:31,970 --> 00:07:34,701
webp, woff, mp4, pdf and zip.

127
00:07:35,033 --> 00:07:39,509
Not cached by default: HTML, JSON,
anything with a Set-Cookie,

128
00:07:39,509 --> 00:07:44,346
or with Cache-Control private, no-
store, no-cache or max-age zero.

129
00:07:44,666 --> 00:07:46,192
If the origin is silent,

130
00:07:46,192 --> 00:07:50,579
the default edge TTL is two hours for
two hundreds and three oh ones,

131
00:07:50,579 --> 00:07:54,457
three minutes for four oh fours, and
the rest are not cached.

132
00:07:54,800 --> 00:07:57,931
The default cache key is scheme,
host, path and query.

133
00:07:58,211 --> 00:08:00,477
Cookies and most headers are not in the key.

134
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:03,906
So two users with different
cookies share one object.

135
00:08:04,186 --> 00:08:07,502
Fine for app dot css, catastrophic for/account.

136
00:08:07,782 --> 00:08:10,685
Vary, or a custom key, is how
you say these differ.

137
00:08:11,633 --> 00:08:14,065
The cf-cache-status header has eight states.

138
00:08:15,100 --> 00:08:18,927
MISS: eligible, not in the cache, so it
was fetched from the origin.

139
00:08:19,266 --> 00:08:21,023
HIT: served from cache, fresh.

140
00:08:21,303 --> 00:08:22,504
No origin hit.

141
00:08:22,833 --> 00:08:26,698
EXPIRED: it was cached, the TTL
passed, so it was re-fetched.

142
00:08:27,033 --> 00:08:31,591
REVALIDATED: the TTL passed, but the
origin answered three oh four,

143
00:08:31,591 --> 00:08:33,632
so the cached copy was reused.

144
00:08:33,912 --> 00:08:34,793
A cheap hit.

145
00:08:35,133 --> 00:08:39,318
UPDATING: stale content is served now,
and refreshed in the background.

146
00:08:39,598 --> 00:08:41,730
Like proxy_cache_use_stale updating.

147
00:08:42,066 --> 00:08:45,281
STALE: the origin is down, so serve stale anyway.

148
00:08:45,561 --> 00:08:46,861
More in session seven.

149
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:50,378
DYNAMIC: not eligible, so it
never looked in the cache.

150
00:08:50,658 --> 00:08:52,260
The default for HTML.

151
00:08:52,600 --> 00:08:57,387
BYPASS: eligible, but the origin said no-
store, or a bypass rule matched.

152
00:08:58,333 --> 00:08:59,976
How do you cache the uncacheable?

153
00:09:00,256 --> 00:09:02,155
Move the personalisation outward.

154
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:09,797
SSI, Server-Side Includes, from NCSA HTTPd one
point zero a one, in nineteen ninety-four.

155
00:09:10,077 --> 00:09:13,080
It predates CGI, and nginx still ships it.

156
00:09:13,360 --> 00:09:17,351
Holes in the HTML are filled by the
server before sending.

157
00:09:17,700 --> 00:09:23,715
ESI, Edge-Side Includes, from Akamai and Oracle,
a W three C note in two thousand one.

158
00:09:23,995 --> 00:09:25,897
The same holes, filled at the edge.

159
00:09:26,177 --> 00:09:30,572
Cache the shared shell everywhere, and assemble
per user at the point of presence.

160
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:32,884
Ajax, around two thousand five.

161
00:09:33,164 --> 00:09:34,669
Holes pushed to the browser.

162
00:09:34,949 --> 00:09:39,179
Cache the body on the CDN, and fetch the
personal bits asynchronously.

163
00:09:39,500 --> 00:09:43,232
And Railgun, from Cloudflare, twenty
thirteen to twenty twenty-four.

164
00:09:43,512 --> 00:09:47,928
Give up on caching the page, and send only
the bytes that changed, a delta.

165
00:09:48,266 --> 00:09:50,609
The arc is: server, edge, browser.

166
00:09:50,889 --> 00:09:53,537
Only the small personal part is recomputed.

167
00:09:54,466 --> 00:09:55,528
ESI, in numbers.

168
00:09:55,866 --> 00:09:57,670
The origin sends one shell,

169
00:09:57,670 --> 00:10:02,078
with ESI include tags for the user
fragment and the cart fragment,

170
00:10:02,078 --> 00:10:08,223
and Cache-Control s-maxage three hundred, plus a
Surrogate-Control header marking it as ESI.

171
00:10:09,266 --> 00:10:13,954
With a hundred logged-in users and a page
of about forty thousand bytes,

172
00:10:13,954 --> 00:10:17,534
if the origin assembles every
page: a hundred requests,

173
00:10:17,534 --> 00:10:21,376
nearly four million bytes from
origin to edge, all dynamic.

174
00:10:21,700 --> 00:10:26,228
With ESI: two hundred one requests, because
each fragment is its own fetch,

175
00:10:26,228 --> 00:10:28,462
but only eighty-three thousand bytes.

176
00:10:28,742 --> 00:10:30,192
Ninety-eight percent less.

177
00:10:30,472 --> 00:10:32,328
And ninety-nine hits, one miss.

178
00:10:32,666 --> 00:10:38,183
The trade: origin bytes drop ninety-eight
percent, the user's download is unchanged,

179
00:10:38,183 --> 00:10:39,891
and origin requests go up.

180
00:10:40,171 --> 00:10:43,400
The load moves from origin
rendering to edge assembly.

181
00:10:44,333 --> 00:10:48,453
Delta compression, from Railgun to
RFC ninety-eight forty-two.

182
00:10:48,733 --> 00:10:52,481
A twenty-four kilobyte news page,
regenerated every minute.

183
00:10:52,800 --> 00:10:57,186
Gzip, with no dictionary: four thousand
two hundred seventy-six bytes,

184
00:10:57,186 --> 00:10:59,066
seventeen point seven percent.

185
00:10:59,400 --> 00:11:04,140
Deflate, with the page from a minute ago as
dictionary: five ninety-eight bytes,

186
00:11:04,140 --> 00:11:05,502
two point five percent.

187
00:11:05,833 --> 00:11:09,840
Zstd with the same dictionary: four
hundred thirty-six bytes.

188
00:11:10,120 --> 00:11:11,508
One point eight percent.

189
00:11:11,833 --> 00:11:15,873
Zstd with a dictionary from an hour
ago: twelve seventy-seven.

190
00:11:16,153 --> 00:11:17,555
Five point three percent.

191
00:11:17,900 --> 00:11:22,622
Railgun kept the last version of each page at
both ends and sent only the delta.

192
00:11:22,902 --> 00:11:25,657
It was retired in January twenty twenty-four,

193
00:11:25,657 --> 00:11:28,840
because the network got fast and
a standard arrived.

194
00:11:29,166 --> 00:11:34,080
That standard is RFC ninety-eight forty-two,
Compression Dictionary Transport,

195
00:11:34,080 --> 00:11:36,222
from September twenty twenty-five.

196
00:11:36,502 --> 00:11:39,104
The browser's previous version is the dictionary.

197
00:11:39,384 --> 00:11:44,075
Headers: Available-Dictionary with a
sha two fifty-six hash,

198
00:11:44,075 --> 00:11:49,860
and Content-Encoding dcb or dcz, for
dictionary compressed brotli or zstd.

199
00:11:50,140 --> 00:11:55,703
It is same-origin and HTTPS only, because
dictionaries leak, as with CRIME.

200
00:11:56,033 --> 00:11:57,938
About ten times better than gzip.

201
00:11:58,218 --> 00:12:02,399
The catch: the dictionary must be
recent, and identical on both ends.

202
00:12:03,333 --> 00:12:04,814
Two classic cache attacks.

203
00:12:05,094 --> 00:12:06,884
A shared cache is a shared weapon.

204
00:12:07,233 --> 00:12:09,979
Cache poisoning, by James Kettle, twenty eighteen.

205
00:12:10,259 --> 00:12:14,990
The origin reflects an unkeyed header, like
X-Forwarded-Host, into the page.

206
00:12:15,270 --> 00:12:19,855
One attacker request caches a script tag
pointing at the attacker's domain,

207
00:12:19,855 --> 00:12:22,055
and every visitor gets it, as a hit.

208
00:12:22,335 --> 00:12:26,858
The fix: drop the unkeyed header at the edge,
add it to the key, or Vary on it.

209
00:12:27,200 --> 00:12:30,726
Cache deception, by Omer Gil,
Black Hat twenty seventeen.

210
00:12:31,006 --> 00:12:34,603
Trick a victim into loading/account/settings/fake
dot css.

211
00:12:34,883 --> 00:12:38,740
The edge sees dot css and caches
the victim's private page.

212
00:12:39,020 --> 00:12:41,782
The attacker fetches the same URL from the cache.

213
00:12:42,062 --> 00:12:44,462
The fix: do not cache by extension alone.

214
00:12:44,742 --> 00:12:47,440
Honour the origin's content type and no-store.

215
00:12:47,766 --> 00:12:50,420
Both are the cache key disagreeing with reality.

216
00:12:51,366 --> 00:12:52,732
Moving bytes faster.

217
00:12:53,012 --> 00:12:55,791
BBR, again, now at CDN scale.

218
00:12:56,071 --> 00:13:01,566
Bottleneck Bandwidth and Round-trip propagation
time, by Cardwell and colleagues at Google,

219
00:13:01,566 --> 00:13:02,471
twenty sixteen.

220
00:13:02,800 --> 00:13:06,208
Loss-based CUBIC fills the pipe
until a drop, then halves.

221
00:13:06,488 --> 00:13:10,981
On long, lossy CDN paths it mistakes
distance for congestion.

222
00:13:11,300 --> 00:13:16,850
BBR measures the bottleneck bandwidth and the
minimum round trip time, and paces to that.

223
00:13:17,130 --> 00:13:19,158
Loss is not a congestion signal.

224
00:13:20,199 --> 00:13:25,831
Google's B4 and YouTube, in twenty sixteen: two to
twenty-five times CUBIC's throughput,

225
00:13:25,831 --> 00:13:29,159
and median round trip time down
fifty-three percent.

226
00:13:29,499 --> 00:13:33,614
Amazon CloudFront in twenty nineteen:
up to twenty-two percent,

227
00:13:33,614 --> 00:13:35,964
and some US mobile networks doubled.

228
00:13:36,299 --> 00:13:42,119
Akamai, November twenty nineteen: BBR on more than
eighty percent of end-user connections.

229
00:13:42,466 --> 00:13:45,023
And a twenty twenty-three web census:

230
00:13:45,023 --> 00:13:51,310
BBR is now the most deployed congestion control
algorithm among the top ten thousand sites.

231
00:13:51,590 --> 00:13:53,385
CUBIC is about seven percent.

232
00:13:53,733 --> 00:13:56,143
The key point: only the sender changes.

233
00:13:56,423 --> 00:13:59,271
No new protocol, no new client, no new router.

234
00:13:59,551 --> 00:14:03,505
A CDN owns the sender on both legs, so
it can just switch it on.

235
00:14:04,433 --> 00:14:06,271
Connection pooling to the origin.

236
00:14:06,551 --> 00:14:12,617
Three hundred visitors, fifty milliseconds between
edge and origin, TLS one point three,

237
00:14:12,617 --> 00:14:16,478
and the origin closes idle
connections after one second.

238
00:14:16,799 --> 00:14:19,969
No pool: three hundred new connections, no reuse,

239
00:14:19,969 --> 00:14:23,850
a mean time to first byte of one
hundred sixty milliseconds.

240
00:14:24,199 --> 00:14:29,437
Thirty-two worker pools: one sixty-four new
connections, forty-five percent reuse,

241
00:14:29,437 --> 00:14:30,139
one twelve.

242
00:14:30,466 --> 00:14:35,627
Eight worker pools: fifty-two new connections,
eighty-three percent reuse, seventy-four.

243
00:14:35,966 --> 00:14:41,261
One shared pool: just four new connections,
ninety-eight point seven percent reuse,

244
00:14:41,261 --> 00:14:42,856
fifty-seven milliseconds.

245
00:14:43,199 --> 00:14:47,819
A pooled connection opened by one worker
can only be reused by that worker.

246
00:14:48,099 --> 00:14:50,249
That is nginx's per-worker pools.

247
00:14:50,529 --> 00:14:51,970
Split pools stay cold.

248
00:14:52,299 --> 00:14:56,707
Cloudflare's Pingora, with a shared pool,
September twenty twenty-two:

249
00:14:56,707 --> 00:14:59,666
one big customer's reuse went from eighty-seven

250
00:14:59,666 --> 00:15:02,688
point one percent to ninety-nine point nine two.

251
00:15:02,968 --> 00:15:06,612
That is a hundred sixty times fewer
new origin connections,

252
00:15:06,612 --> 00:15:10,874
and four hundred thirty-four years of
handshake time saved every day.

253
00:15:11,199 --> 00:15:13,542
Amortise the setup, across a continent.

254
00:15:13,822 --> 00:15:16,578
The setup is a two round trip TLS handshake.

255
00:15:17,499 --> 00:15:18,972
Multiplexing and shielding.

256
00:15:19,299 --> 00:15:23,089
CDNs use HTTP two on both legs in principle,

257
00:15:23,089 --> 00:15:27,998
but most CDN to origin hops are
still HTTP one point one.

258
00:15:28,278 --> 00:15:32,258
cloudflared's http2Origin
setting defaults to false.

259
00:15:32,538 --> 00:15:34,846
Pooling is what actually saves you today.

260
00:15:35,166 --> 00:15:39,535
A tiered cache, or origin shield: without
it, three hundred edges miss,

261
00:15:39,535 --> 00:15:43,166
and send three hundred origin
requests for the same object.

262
00:15:43,446 --> 00:15:44,969
A built-in thundering herd.

263
00:15:45,249 --> 00:15:48,829
With it, edges fetch from a regional
parent, which fetches once.

264
00:15:49,166 --> 00:15:53,805
Request collapsing at one edge: fifty
users, a cold cache, one object.

265
00:15:54,085 --> 00:15:56,665
Without collapsing, fifty origin fetches.

266
00:15:56,945 --> 00:15:59,778
With it, one, and the other
forty-nine wait, then hit.

267
00:16:00,058 --> 00:16:02,713
nginx's version is proxy_cache_lock.

268
00:16:03,033 --> 00:16:07,278
Collapsing within one point of presence,
plus tiered caching across them,

269
00:16:07,278 --> 00:16:10,243
means never fetching the same
object twice at once.

270
00:16:10,523 --> 00:16:14,992
The CDN protects the origin during a
spike, instead of amplifying it.

271
00:16:15,933 --> 00:16:17,902
Now, everything that is not speed.

272
00:16:18,182 --> 00:16:20,972
All requests are coming to the CDN anyway.

273
00:16:21,299 --> 00:16:27,760
So the CDN grows a WAF, that blocks SQL injection,
cross-site scripting and path traversal.

274
00:16:28,040 --> 00:16:28,950
Rate limiting.

275
00:16:29,230 --> 00:16:30,546
DDoS absorption.

276
00:16:30,826 --> 00:16:36,300
And an API gateway: authentication, adding and
stripping headers, routing, and fallback.

277
00:16:36,633 --> 00:16:38,170
Speed got you to adopt it.

278
00:16:38,450 --> 00:16:40,319
Control is why you cannot leave.

279
00:16:41,266 --> 00:16:43,234
A WAF is a denylist of spellings.

280
00:16:43,514 --> 00:16:45,238
The bug still lives in your origin.

281
00:16:46,266 --> 00:16:50,700
The classic tautology, quote OR one
equals one, is blocked by the rule.

282
00:16:51,033 --> 00:16:54,825
Replace the spaces with SQL
comments, and five rows leak.

283
00:16:55,166 --> 00:16:58,812
Add normalisation, stripping the
comments, and it is blocked again.

284
00:16:59,133 --> 00:17:03,084
But a quote and a comment, with no OR,
UNION or digits, leaks again.

285
00:17:03,433 --> 00:17:07,226
The arms race is normalisation,
against the number of spellings.

286
00:17:07,506 --> 00:17:09,044
It is never won outright.

287
00:17:09,366 --> 00:17:13,179
A WAF buys time, and blocks mass-
scanned known payloads,

288
00:17:13,179 --> 00:17:15,654
like Log4Shell and the OWASP top ten.

289
00:17:15,934 --> 00:17:21,099
The real fix is to bind parameters, using
parameterised queries, in the origin.

290
00:17:21,433 --> 00:17:24,581
Cloudflare gives the Free Managed
Ruleset on all plans.

291
00:17:24,861 --> 00:17:26,610
The OWASP core set needs Pro.

292
00:17:27,533 --> 00:17:28,443
Rate limiting.

293
00:17:28,723 --> 00:17:31,599
A hundred per ten seconds has
five different meanings.

294
00:17:32,633 --> 00:17:37,149
Fixed window: a hundred requests at nine
point nine nine nine seconds,

295
00:17:37,149 --> 00:17:39,858
and a hundred more at ten point oh oh one.

296
00:17:40,138 --> 00:17:41,734
Two hundred in one second.

297
00:17:42,066 --> 00:17:44,986
A sliding log, which is exact, allows a hundred.

298
00:17:45,333 --> 00:17:48,638
A sliding window estimate allows
about a hundred and four.

299
00:17:48,966 --> 00:17:51,477
A token bucket allows a hundred and nine.

300
00:17:51,799 --> 00:17:54,430
A leaky bucket with no burst allows ten,

301
00:17:54,430 --> 00:17:58,046
but refuses ninety-seven percent
of a normal page load,

302
00:17:58,046 --> 00:18:01,203
because a browser opens thirty requests at once.

303
00:18:01,533 --> 00:18:05,750
Cloudflare's answer, in twenty seventeen,
is the sliding window estimate.

304
00:18:06,030 --> 00:18:09,307
Weight the previous window by how
much of it still overlaps.

305
00:18:09,587 --> 00:18:10,995
Two counters per client.

306
00:18:11,275 --> 00:18:13,658
Across four hundred million requests,

307
00:18:13,658 --> 00:18:18,294
only zero point zero zero three percent
were wrongly allowed or blocked.

308
00:18:18,633 --> 00:18:21,857
The free plan offers only the ten
second fixed window.

309
00:18:22,137 --> 00:18:26,388
nginx's limit_req has a burst
parameter for exactly this reason.

310
00:18:26,668 --> 00:18:29,059
And counts are per data centre, not global.

311
00:18:29,339 --> 00:18:33,626
A botnet across three hundred thirty cities
gets three hundred thirty budgets.

312
00:18:34,566 --> 00:18:35,320
DDoS.

313
00:18:35,600 --> 00:18:38,141
The one thing you genuinely cannot do yourself.

314
00:18:38,466 --> 00:18:40,721
Thirty-one point four terabits per second.

315
00:18:41,001 --> 00:18:46,057
The largest DDoS on record, mitigated by
Cloudflare in December twenty twenty-five.

316
00:18:46,337 --> 00:18:49,382
The Aisuru botnet, one to four million devices.

317
00:18:49,662 --> 00:18:53,283
It lasted thirty-five seconds, and
was mitigated automatically.

318
00:18:53,633 --> 00:18:56,129
Cloudflare's capacity is about five hundred

319
00:18:56,129 --> 00:18:59,265
terabits across more than three
hundred thirty cities.

320
00:18:59,545 --> 00:19:05,493
Volumetric floods are won with more capacity, and
anycast splits the flood across locations.

321
00:19:05,773 --> 00:19:07,371
Your origin has one pipe.

322
00:19:07,699 --> 00:19:13,261
The catch: one network absorbing everyone's attacks
is everyone's single point of failure.

323
00:19:13,541 --> 00:19:16,260
Fastly, the eighth of June twenty twenty-one:

324
00:19:16,260 --> 00:19:21,155
one customer's config change sent eighty-five
percent of the network into errors.

325
00:19:21,435 --> 00:19:24,519
Reddit, Spotify and gov dot uk went down.

326
00:19:24,799 --> 00:19:26,422
Forty-nine minutes to recover.

327
00:19:27,366 --> 00:19:30,799
The edge as an API gateway, using JWTs.

328
00:19:31,833 --> 00:19:33,539
No token: four oh one.

329
00:19:33,866 --> 00:19:39,553
A valid JWT: the edge sets an X-User
header, and the origin returns data.

330
00:19:39,899 --> 00:19:41,946
An expired token: four oh one.

331
00:19:42,266 --> 00:19:45,163
A forged token with algorithm none: refused.

332
00:19:45,443 --> 00:19:47,489
Never let the token pick the algorithm.

333
00:19:47,833 --> 00:19:52,355
A client that sends its own X-User admin
header: ignored, or overwritten.

334
00:19:52,699 --> 00:19:56,868
But an attacker who hits the origin IP
directly gets everything.

335
00:19:57,148 --> 00:20:01,147
The gateway is only real if the origin
cannot be reached directly.

336
00:20:01,466 --> 00:20:06,294
The fix: mutual TLS, called
authenticated origin pulls, or a tunnel,

337
00:20:06,294 --> 00:20:11,477
so that the origin only answers clients
that hold the edge's certificate.

338
00:20:11,757 --> 00:20:14,675
And practise header hygiene: strip X-Powered-By.

339
00:20:15,599 --> 00:20:16,836
Cloudflare hands-on.

340
00:20:17,116 --> 00:20:18,806
Some demos need no account.

341
00:20:19,133 --> 00:20:25,372
No account needed: the trace endpoint, dig
CHAOS, and curl -capital I for cf-ray,

342
00:20:25,372 --> 00:20:27,915
cf-cache-status, age and alt-svc.

343
00:20:28,233 --> 00:20:31,424
Compare TLS time from Mumbai and from Virginia,

344
00:20:31,424 --> 00:20:35,430
using curl with time_appconnect
against regional endpoints.

345
00:20:35,766 --> 00:20:40,009
And a Quick Tunnel: cloudflared tunnel
--url, pointing at localhost.

346
00:20:40,289 --> 00:20:42,429
You get a trycloudflare.com address.

347
00:20:42,709 --> 00:20:44,357
No account, no DNS.

348
00:20:44,637 --> 00:20:48,465
Limits: two hundred in-flight, no
server-sent events, testing only.

349
00:20:48,745 --> 00:20:53,747
The echo server prints CF-
Connecting-IP, CF-Ray and CDN-Loop,

350
00:20:53,747 --> 00:20:56,570
and shows very few TCP connections.

351
00:20:56,850 --> 00:20:57,847
That is the pool.

352
00:20:59,966 --> 00:21:04,572
On the dashboard, free plan: the orange
cloud versus the grey cloud,

353
00:21:04,572 --> 00:21:07,417
which hides your origin IP behind anycast.

354
00:21:07,697 --> 00:21:13,061
Cache an HTML page, and watch miss, hit, purge,
miss, with the Age header climbing.

355
00:21:13,341 --> 00:21:14,595
A WAF custom rule.

356
00:21:14,875 --> 00:21:20,124
A rate limit that passes about twenty requests and
then returns four twenty-nine, per colo.

357
00:21:20,404 --> 00:21:22,891
A transform rule to strip X-Powered-By.

358
00:21:23,171 --> 00:21:27,563
And a Worker, deployed with wrangler, up to a
hundred thousand requests a day.

359
00:21:28,499 --> 00:21:29,267
Workers.

360
00:21:29,547 --> 00:21:31,787
Session three's question, answered again.

361
00:21:32,133 --> 00:21:34,629
CGI forked a process per request.

362
00:21:34,966 --> 00:21:36,718
FastCGI kept it warm.

363
00:21:37,066 --> 00:21:39,632
The nginx event loop removed the worker.

364
00:21:39,966 --> 00:21:44,037
Workers: the runtime is already resident
in every point of presence,

365
00:21:44,037 --> 00:21:45,833
and your code just runs in it.

366
00:21:46,113 --> 00:21:47,946
Amortise the setup, at the edge.

367
00:21:48,266 --> 00:21:51,205
A Worker is a V eight isolate, not a process.

368
00:21:51,485 --> 00:21:55,575
It starts in about five milliseconds,
using about three megabytes,

369
00:21:55,575 --> 00:21:59,417
against a container's thirty-five
megabytes plus a cold start.

370
00:21:59,766 --> 00:22:05,778
An example worker rate limits by IP, verifies
a JWT with WebCrypto, sets X-User,

371
00:22:05,778 --> 00:22:10,287
and fetches the origin with failover
from primary to backup.

372
00:22:10,567 --> 00:22:12,478
It is tested locally in workerd.

373
00:22:12,758 --> 00:22:16,449
The ESI worker had a framing bug:
where does this element end?

374
00:22:16,729 --> 00:22:19,109
That is why HTMLRewriter exists.

375
00:22:20,033 --> 00:22:20,663
The bill.

376
00:22:20,943 --> 00:22:21,961
What you hand over.

377
00:22:22,999 --> 00:22:24,707
Your TLS private keys.

378
00:22:24,987 --> 00:22:28,790
The edge terminates HTTPS, so it
can read every byte.

379
00:22:29,133 --> 00:22:30,567
A single point of failure.

380
00:22:30,847 --> 00:22:32,886
Fastly, June twenty twenty-one.

381
00:22:33,233 --> 00:22:35,096
The power to see and shape traffic.

382
00:22:35,376 --> 00:22:39,918
Domain fronting, which hid censored
traffic behind innocent domains,

383
00:22:39,918 --> 00:22:44,995
was banned by AWS and Google in twenty
eighteen, Azure in twenty twenty-two,

384
00:22:44,995 --> 00:22:47,800
and Fastly in February twenty twenty-four.

385
00:22:48,133 --> 00:22:49,775
And lock-in by architecture.

386
00:22:50,055 --> 00:22:54,691
WAF, rate limits, auth and Workers at the
edge make it your security perimeter,

387
00:22:54,691 --> 00:22:55,983
not a swappable cache.

388
00:22:56,899 --> 00:22:58,455
Four ideas from this session.

389
00:22:58,799 --> 00:22:59,302
One.

390
00:22:59,582 --> 00:23:01,304
Move the work, or move the data.

391
00:23:01,584 --> 00:23:03,310
Content closer, with a cache.

392
00:23:03,590 --> 00:23:07,372
Or personalisation outward: SSI, ESI, Ajax.

393
00:23:07,699 --> 00:23:08,236
Two.

394
00:23:08,516 --> 00:23:11,255
The edge is wherever all the traffic converges.

395
00:23:11,535 --> 00:23:16,862
The firewall, the rate limiter, the DDoS scrubber,
auth and code all followed the traffic.

396
00:23:17,199 --> 00:23:17,786
Three.

397
00:23:18,066 --> 00:23:21,111
It is all sessions two to five,
rented by the city.

398
00:23:21,391 --> 00:23:27,198
Reverse proxy, pooling, ETag revalidation,
BBR, multiplexing, consistent hashing.

399
00:23:27,533 --> 00:23:28,086
Four.

400
00:23:28,366 --> 00:23:30,196
Concentration cuts both ways.

401
00:23:30,476 --> 00:23:32,388
It absorbs thirty-one terabits,

402
00:23:32,388 --> 00:23:36,399
and one config bug takes eighty-five
percent of the network down.

403
00:23:37,333 --> 00:23:40,760
Here is what the quiz is most likely
to ask from session six.

404
00:24:06,499 --> 00:24:07,729
That is session six.

405
00:24:08,009 --> 00:24:10,760
Next up, session seven: building for failures.

